From b9125242b4dfdfb93025868442ff0ee1cf417137 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Homies Dev <dev@homies.local>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 07:08:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] fix: one mechanism for the API base, replacing three that
 disagreed
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Symptom: `npm start` with a local server running still showed a
multi-second navbar delay and, on a flaky network, Proxy error EAI_AGAIN.
Running `node server.js` locally had no effect whatsoever.

Cause: API URLs were resolved three different ways.

  1. client/package.json "proxy" -> the deployed Render backend
  2. client/vercel.json rewrites -> the deployed Render backend
  3. HostelsSection.js checked REACT_APP_API_URL by hand, while the other
     21 axios calls used relative paths

The proxy is the damaging one. In development every relative /api call was
forwarded to the *deployed* backend, so a developer editing a route
handler locally saw no change, and every request paid a free-tier
cold-start penalty against a remote host. When DNS to that host failed,
the whole app failed, with a local server sitting idle on port 5000.

Now there is one mechanism. axios.defaults.baseURL is set once from
REACT_APP_API_URL: unset means relative/same-origin, set means that
origin. client/.env.development points at http://localhost:5000 and is
committed deliberately — it holds no secrets and it is what makes local
development behave the way everyone already assumed it did.
.env.production.example documents the deployed value.

Calling the API cross-origin means the server must permit it, so CORS is
now handled properly rather than sidestepped by proxying. x-auth-token is
named explicitly in allowedHeaders; it is not a CORS-safelisted header,
so without that every authenticated request fails at preflight. The
origin allowlist comes from CORS_ORIGINS and defaults to permissive,
which is right for local work and acceptable for this deployment, where
the API exposes nothing beyond what a logged-in student already sees.

Rejected: keeping the proxy and only changing its target to localhost.
That fixes the symptom and leaves three mechanisms in place, one of which
is invisible in the source and applies only in development. The next
person to deploy would hit the same confusion.

Two further fixes ride along because they are in the same file and the
same deployment story:

- /uploads is now mounted explicitly. Multer writes to
  client/public/uploads, but production served client/build and nothing
  mounted client/public, so an avatar uploaded after deployment was
  written where no route could reach it and came back as index.html. It
  appeared to work only because `react-scripts build` copies public/ into
  build/, so images committed before a deploy were served and images
  uploaded after one were not. This does not survive a restart on an
  ephemeral filesystem — that needs a mounted disk or object storage —
  but it is the difference between working for the life of the instance
  and never working at all.

- GET /api/health reports environment and database state. It is the
  quickest way to confirm which backend you have actually reached, and it
  can be used to wake a spun-down free-tier instance before a demo rather
  than during one.

Deployment configuration is updated to match. vercel.json no longer
proxies /api (redundant now, and it would mask a misconfigured
REACT_APP_API_URL) and keeps only the SPA fallback.
client/public/_redirects adds the same fallback for Cloudflare Pages,
without which a hard load of /dashboard returns the host's own 404 and
the app never boots — indistinguishable from a broken deployment.

`npm run doctor` now checks this mechanism instead of the proxy, and
fails loudly if a "proxy" entry reappears, since a leftover one would
silently override REACT_APP_API_URL.

Does not fix: the cold-start delay when you genuinely are talking to a
spun-down free instance. That is a hosting tier characteristic. /api/health
gives you a way to warm it deliberately.
